# Summary
Introduced by the new scripts/uat-multihost-raft file. The script deploys authenticated TCP endpoints but uses deterministic credential contents instead of cryptographically random secrets.
scripts/uat-multihost-raft creates the Raft, storage-node, and admin HMAC credential files by formatting a fixed string containing run_id, role, and node number. The run_id is not treated as secret: it is based on timestamp, PID, and Bash RANDOM, is embedded in paths/unit names, and is printed to stdout. As a result, the supposedly authenticated real-host soak can be accessed by an attacker who learns or guesses the run_id and can reach the configured TCP endpoints. The attacker can derive credential material such as argmin-multihost-<run_id>-admin-1-secret and forge authenticated control-plane/admin or Raft-peer requests for the UAT cluster. Secrets should be generated with a cryptographic random source such as openssl rand or /dev/urandom, and the run identifier should not be used as credential entropy.

# Resolution
Fixed by commit https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/ac250a6c5d986fc4cce339c0d3e49b443f9bf96b (`ac250a6c Randomize multihost soak credentials`).

The finding was valid but limited to the multihost UAT harness rather than the production runtime. The harness now generates every Raft-peer, storage-node, and admin HMAC credential independently with `openssl rand -hex 32`. Credential material no longer contains or derives from the public `run_id`.

Each credential file is created under a per-file `umask 077`, so secret material is restricted from initial publication rather than tightened after creation. Before deployment, the harness also fails closed unless every generated secret is exactly 32 random bytes encoded as 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters and all nine role/node credentials are unique.

Verification for the fix:
